WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:05.680
One hallmark of writing any code in any app&nbsp;
is to protect the code as much as possible.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:12.960
To do so, we want to, when necessary, not have&nbsp;
SQL statements written directly in apps, such as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:18.560
C#-based apps, for example. One can&nbsp;
accomplish this by creating and executing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:25.600
a stored procedure. A stored procedure is a saved&nbsp;
query, but unlike a view, it can accept both input&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:30.880
and output parameters. Let's take a look at this&nbsp;
stored procedure. It still has a SELECT statement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:37.600
just like a view does, but the similarities to&nbsp;
a view stop there. This process does start with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:44.800
the CREATE PROCEDURE statement. Then look at&nbsp;
these two items with at symbols (@). These are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:50.720
used as parameters, which are values that pass&nbsp;
through a query. The EmployeeID parameter is an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:57.440
input parameter. How do we know this? It is not&nbsp;
specified as an output parameter, so the employee&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:03.600
ID is something a person will need to input when&nbsp;
executing this stored procedure. The DepartmentID&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:09.920
parameter is an output parameter. These parameters&nbsp;
are used in apps to return a value the app will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:16.720
then use later. The SELECT statement says that we&nbsp;
want to select the DepartmentID as a parameter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:25.520
an output parameter, from the&nbsp;
EmployeeDepartmentHistory table, where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:32.800
the BusinessEntityID is equal to the EmployeeID&nbsp;
parameter, the input that we specified. If someone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:39.360
were to execute this stored procedure and enter&nbsp;
16 for an Employee ID, the query would return the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:46.400
employee's department. All that said, an output&nbsp;
parameter does not show the DepartmentID here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:51.760
Rather, the output parameter determines the order&nbsp;
in which outputs are sent to the app. If there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:57.680
multiple outputs, output one will be sent to an&nbsp;
app first, followed by output two, and so on. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:04.960
will run this code to build the stored procedure.&nbsp;
Then, I will select and run this EXEC statement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:15.600
which executes the code. We get a message&nbsp;
that the command was completed successfully,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:20.400
but no results show because they&nbsp;
are meant to be used within an app.

